Changelog: 13-10-2026 02:34:21

Release 0.1.4.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 02:23:15

The ported code's origin is named only where licensing needs it (owner): Console Link's LICENSE and the License sections of the README and the manual chapter, which now also say which files are the modified ones (those marked AGPL-3.0-only) and that the code calls their origin "the reference". Everywhere else, comments, docstrings, test names, the vectors' descriptions, this CHANGELOG and MIGRATION.md, it is "the reference". Messages for the user no longer mention it at all: the made-up session ID line ("which the console accepts for the stream"), the network test's fallback ("safe values instead"), the sign-in dialog and the manual's account ID and sign-in paragraphs.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 02:18:03

The video decoder (iteration 2, owner's choice 13-10-2026: FFmpeg bundled in the wheel CI builds, so installing stays pip only). native/ffmpeg/build.sh builds FFmpeg 7.1.2 from its pinned sources (signature checked when pinned, checksum checked on every build), static and LGPL only: the H.264 and HEVC decoders with VA-API, nothing else; libva stays the system's, linked dynamically (without its headers, as on Bazzite, they come from libva 2.22.0's pinned sources). The cargo feature "decoder" links it (build.rs, decoder.c: a small C interface over libavcodec, decoder.rs over that); Python gets Decoder, DECODER and FFMPEG_VERSION. The stream opens the decoder at STREAMINFO and decodes every frame; the resolution's header goes in front of the next frame (the reference sends it alone, which FFmpeg's H.264 decoder refuses). VA-API when the GPU has a video decoder, else software: the BC-250 (Cyan Skillfish) has no VCN, so on the board it is software; measured there, one thread with FFmpeg's assembly: 3.6 ms per 720p H.264 frame, 6.6 ms at 1080p, 13.9 ms for 1080p HEVC (without nasm 6.3, 13.2 and 38.5 ms). Test stream reports the frames decoded and how; the log names the decoder and the time per frame. install.sh installs the wheel a release brings (native/wheel), and from a clone builds the core with the decoder when build.sh has built FFmpeg. FFmpeg's LGPL text in native/ffmpeg. Tests: H.264 and HEVC clips made with x264 and x265 (tests/video_vectors.json) decode to the same pictures as the system's FFmpeg, in Rust and Python; the fake console streams those clips in the codec the launch spec asks for.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 02:02:34

Release 0.1.3.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 01:53:29

The video frames in the native core (iteration 2): fec.rs, the Cauchy Reed-Solomon FEC over GF(2^8) that the reference gets from Jerasure, written from its definition (polynomial 0x11d, Jerasure's original Cauchy matrix) and checked against eight frames recorded by the reference (tests/fec_vectors.json, from its test/fec_test_cases.inl; our encoder also gives the console's FEC units); frame.rs, the frame processor (units into frames, FEC when units are missing) and the packet counts; video.rs, the video receiver (frame order, flush when enough units are there, CORRUPTFRAME for frames that never came or could not be rebuilt); and the congestion reports every 200 ms, the loss reported at most 5 %. The connection starts all of it with start_stream at STREAMINFO; from then on video comes out as whole frames, and the Python side sends the CORRUPTFRAME reports. The reference's arithmetic kept as it is: a frame without FEC units counts one FEC unit expected, and the audio counts take the sequence difference as lost when more packets came than it. Differences: a frame whose first packet is invalid is empty here (the reference keeps the last frame's state). Not yet: the reference's look into the bitstream (a missing reference frame swapped for an older one, a key frame asked for after a failed FEC), which comes with the decoder. Test stream now reports frames, frames rebuilt with FEC, frames lost and the video bitrate; the log has the video receiver's counts. Python: fec_encode and fec_decode, Takion.start_stream and video_stats, the events "frame", "profile" and "corrupt". Tests: the FEC, frame and video modules, a connection test with a lost unit, and the fake console sends real frames with FEC units and can lose units and frames.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 01:27:26

Release 0.1.2.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 01:25:57

Renamed from BC250 Remote Play to BC250 Console Link (owner, 13-10-2026), so the app's name no longer overlaps with the console maker's product: folder apps/console-link, package bc250_console_link, app ID bc250-console-link (launcher, menu entry, ~/.local/share and ~/.config folders), images bc250-console-link.png, native module bc250_console_link_core (crate bc250-console-link-core), tags console-link-v<x.y.z>. The console's own feature keeps its name Remote Play wherever the app refers to it (its menus, the protocol and its version). Moving over: install.sh removes BC250 Remote Play's program files and moves its settings and registered consoles to ~/.config/bc250-console-link (the .ini renamed with it), and the app does the same at its first start when the new folder does not exist yet, so a registered console needs no new registration. Tests: the move in test_consoles.py and in the installer test.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 01:15:00

A disclaimer in the README and the manual (owner): an independent project, not affiliated with or endorsed by the makers of PlayStation; PlayStation, PS4, PS5 and PSN are trademarks of their owner.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 01:06:19

The app, its messages, the sign-in dialog and the manual name PSN and PlayStation only (owner: no other company name anywhere): "Open the PlayStation sign-in", errors such as "PSN did not accept the code". Only the sign-in service's own address keeps its domain, as the sign-in needs it. The manual's troubleshooting adds a console that was switched off completely (the owner's PS4 after a release test; fixed with the rest-mode settings).

Changelog: 13-10-2026 01:00:52

The main window names PSN and PlayStation only (owner): the registration hint says the PlayStation sign-in, the footer "Talks to consoles and PSN only when you press a button", and the log "Asking PSN for the account ID".

Changelog: 13-10-2026 00:47:43

The network test before the stream, Senkusha (senkusha.py, from the reference's senkusha.c): a Takion connection of version 7 to UDP 9297 without crypto, the protocol request and BIG, ten pings for the round trip, then a binary search for the MTU in (the console sends video packets of the size asked) and out (the board sends pings of that size). Test stream runs it first and puts its MTU and round trip into the launch spec (MTU in) and the BIG's split (the smaller MTU); when it fails, the stream uses the reference's values for that case (1454 bytes, 1 ms), as the reference does. Its messages equal the reference's nanopb encodings (tests/stream_vectors.json): the reference's MTU command sets num without its has-flag, so num is never sent, and neither is it here. The native core has the version 7 header (v7_av_header, equal to the reference's takion.c, which writes the key position in the machine's byte order where every other field is big-endian), send_raw for the pings, and Takion connections without crypto, where video and audio are not held for keys. As in the reference, an MTU-out search that does not start at 1454 ends at 1453 at most. Tests: the console's side of the network test (SenkushaConsole in tests/fake_stream.py, with MTU limits standing in for the network), tests/test_senkusha.py, the job with the network test and without it, and the v7 header in Rust and from Python.

